  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DG ECHO PILOT PROGRAMMATIC PARTNERSHIP (PPP)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The PPP project is important because it strengthens Uganda Red Cross Society&apos;s capacity to respond to the complex humanitarian challenges arising from hosting a large refugee population, managing internal displacement, and addressing the impacts of climate-related disasters and recurrent disease outbreaks.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The action will focus on 4 main pillars of intervention: 
1) DRM 
2) Epidemic, Pandemic Preparedness and Response
3) People on the move
4) Cash and Voucher Assistance (CVA); and
5) Community Engagement and Accountability (CEA)</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project has, since its inception, been implemented in Amolatar (Apac), Kaberamaido, Kayunga and Nakasongola districts i.e Lake Kyoga region, and Teso Region specifically Soroti, for the regional warehouse. In addition, the project expanded in Year 2 to include Bunyangabu, Kabarole, Kasese, Ntoroko, and Western Region/Rukungiri.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This Action focuses predominantly on the response capacity of OPM, DDMC, DTF, VHTs, partners and URCS at all levels. Based on the objectives of each activity the most relevant staff and volunteers will be selected based on their roles and responsibilities in the organisation while trying to strength the gender and other diversity balance.  The selection criteria for multi-purpose cash and/or NFI distribution will be informed by relevant assessments (needs assessments, RAM etc) based on the socio economic and vulnerability indicators of the community and will be validated by the DDMC, sub-county DMC, local leaders as well as the community members.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project in Uganda is part of the 3-year Global ECHO-funded and IFRC led programme implemented in 25 countries. This project specifically channels funds to Belgian Red Cross Flanders (BRC-FL) for technical and managerial support on first aid response, contributing to the with the aim of strengthening of URCS&apos;s multi-hazard preparedness and response system from national, through district, down to community level, building linkages between EW/EA for a timely disaster preparedness, forecast-based action, and response.</narrative>
  </description>
